I’ve been thinking about the discussion around ADM access, database and storage connectivity, and the friction the current model is creating for the team.

I don’t think the right answer is to weaken the security requirements or eliminate the separation between standard and privileged access. Instead, I think there is an opportunity to redesign the way we work around those controls so the security model remains intact while the delivery experience becomes much more efficient.

At a high level, I’d suggest we separate our recurring activities into three categories:

Normal day-to-day client and analytical work

Privileged administrative activity

Automated or system-driven activity

From there, we can define which identity, permissions, and working environment should be used for each type of activity.

The goal would be to keep routine work such as querying approved client datasets, accessing scoped storage, running analysis, and using approved development tools within a normal project workspace, while reserving ADM/PIM access for true administrative functions such as provisioning resources, changing permissions, modifying infrastructure, or managing security configuration.

I also think there is an opportunity to introduce more project-specific access and potentially provisioned workspaces so teams are not constantly switching identities, browser profiles, and authentication contexts simply to perform routine engagement work.

This would allow us to preserve the hard security boundaries while reducing the operational friction that is beginning to affect delivery.

Rather than try to solve this one authentication issue at a time, I’d be happy to help map a representative engagement end to end and identify where we can separate normal data-plane activity from control-plane administration, where project-level access can be standardized, and where automation or dedicated workload identities could remove unnecessary manual steps.

I think this could give us a much cleaner operating model that is both more secure and better suited to the way our teams actually work today.

Happy to put together a short working session and walk through a proposed framework.

